  "question": "<p>State the Mangasarian-Fromovitz Constraint Qualification (MFCQ) and compare it with LICQ. Why is MFCQ weaker than LICQ?</p>",
  "solution": "<p><b>Explanation:</b><br>1. <b>Definition of MFCQ:</b><br>The Mangasarian-Fromovitz Constraint Qualification holds at $x^*$ if: <ol>   <li>The equality constraint gradients $\\{\\nabla h_i(x^*)\\}_{i=1}^p$ are linearly independent.   <li>There exists a direction $d \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ such that:   $$\\nabla h_i(x^*)^\\top d = 0 \\quad \\forall i = 1, \\dots, p,$$   $$\\nabla g_j(x^*)^\\top d \\lt  0 \\quad \\forall j \\in \\mathcal{A}(x^*) \\quad (\\text{strictly negative!}).$$ </ol></p><p>2. <b>Comparison with LICQ:</b></p><p>• LICQ requires linear independence of ALL active constraints (equalities + active inequalities). <br>• MFCQ does NOT require active inequality constraint gradients to be linearly independent; it only requires the existence of a single strictly interior direction $d$. <br>• If LICQ holds, MFCQ automatically holds. However, MFCQ can hold even when active inequality gradients are linearly dependent. <br>• <b>Significance:</b> MFCQ is equivalent to the set of Lagrange multipliers being non-empty and <b>compact</b> (bounded). LICQ is stronger and guarantees <b>uniqueness</b> of the Lagrange multipliers.</p>",
