  "question": "<p>Why does the First-Order Necessary Condition for a constrained local minimum state that $-\\nabla f(x^*) \\in N_\\Omega(x^*)$?</p>",
  "solution": "<p><b>Explanation:</b><br>Let $x^* \\in \\Omega$ be a local minimum of $f$. For any feasible direction $d \\in T_\\Omega(x^*)$, there cannot be descent along $d$. Thus, the directional derivative must be non-negative: $$\\nabla f(x^*)^\\top d \\ge 0 \\quad \\text{for all } d \\in T_\\Omega(x^*).$$ Multiply by $-1$: $$(-\\nabla f(x^*))^\\top d \\le 0 \\quad \\text{for all } d \\in T_\\Omega(x^*).$$ By definition of the normal cone: $$N_\\Omega(x^*) = \\{ v \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : v^\\top d \\le 0 \\text{ for all } d \\in T_\\Omega(x^*) \\}.$$ Therefore, the negative gradient $-\\nabla f(x^*)$ satisfies the exact defining inequality of the normal cone: $$-\\nabla f(x^*) \\in N_\\Omega(x^*).$$ Geometrically, this means that the negative gradient points into the normal cone, which prevents any feasible movement from reducing the objective value.</p>",
