  "question": "<p>How does the variational inequality $(y - x^*)^\\top (x - x^*) \\le 0$ define a strictly separating hyperplane between $y$ and $C$?</p>",
  "solution": "<p><b>Derivation:</b><br>Let $x^* = \\Pi_C(y)$ with $y \\notin C$. Then $y - x^* \\ne 0$. Define $a = y - x^* \\ne 0$. The variational inequality states: $$a^\\top (x - x^*) \\le 0 \\implies a^\\top x \\le a^\\top x^* \\quad \\text{for all } x \\in C.$$ Now evaluate the linear functional at $y$: $$a^\\top y = a^\\top (x^* + a) = a^\\top x^* + \\|a\\|^2.$$ Since $y \\notin C$, $a = y - x^* \\ne 0$, so $\\|a\\|^2 > 0$. Therefore: $$a^\\top y > a^\\top x^* \\ge a^\\top x \\quad \\text{for all } x \\in C.$$ Choose the separating constant $\\beta = a^\\top x^* + \\frac{1}{2}\\|a\\|^2$. Then: $$a^\\top x \\le a^\\top x^* \\lt  \\beta \\lt  a^\\top y.$$ Thus, the hyperplane $\\{z \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : a^\\top z = \\beta\\}$ strictly separates the point $y$ from the closed convex set $C$.</p>",
